          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Dwyer-Kan fundamental simplicial groupoid
            (often “loop groupoid”) &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#DwyerKan84"}> Dwyer &amp; Kan (1984), §3.1
            \&amp;rbrack; of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+groupoid"} simplicial groupoid
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            and whose
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom+object"} hom
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            between a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
            of such picks up the composable sequences of higher dimensional simplices, where the zeroth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex"} vertex
            is thought of as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            and the first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex"} vertex
            as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
          %p
            So the “loop”-terminology is a misnomer: It really refers to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+homotopy+theory"} simplicial
            version of a
            = succeed "-construction" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+groupoid"} path groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid"}
              fundamental
              = succeed "-groupoid" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+groupoid"} simplicial groupoid
          %p
            But applied to the special case of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+simplicial+set"} reduced simplicial set
            this construction reduces to the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+loop+group"} Kan loop group
            construction, which is properly a model for a
            = succeed "-construction." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
          %p
            The Dwyer-Kan groupoid construction is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+Quillen+functor"} left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            to a groupoidal
            = succeed "-construction)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+classifying+space"} simplicial classifying space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+groupoids"} model structure on simplicial groupoids
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#DwyerKan84"}> Dwyer &amp; Kan (1984), Thm. 3.3
            \&amp;rbrack;, and as such exhibits the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+groupoids"} simplicial groupoids
            as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} equivalent
            incarnation of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+homotopy+theory"} classical homotopy theory
          %h2#Definition Definitions
          %p
            There are different choices of how to define a DK-type loop groupoid functor, depending, for instance, on which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-simplex"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-simplex
            of a simpilcial set are to be regarded as the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target"} target
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            in the loop groupoid. In the original definition
            %a{:href => "#DwyerKan84"} Dwyer &amp; Kan (1984), §3.1
            took the direction of the morphism to point from the
            = succeed "st" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \to 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+spaces"} Segal spaces
            etc. is to instead take
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to 1
          %p
            Moreover, apparently the original definition in
            %a{:href => "#DwyerKan84"} Dwyer &amp; Kan (1984), §3.1
            had a mistake in its definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0
            (according to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Ehlers91"} Ehlers (1991), p. 10
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Ehlers91"} Ehlers (1991), pp. 10
          %p
            Other definitions have been given, too (all necessarily isomorphic to each after their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjointness
            to the
            = succeed "-construction" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+classifying+space"} simplicial classifying space
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#UniquenessOfAdjoints"} uniqueness of adjoint
          %p
            In any case, the DK loop groupoid construction is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
